nobody likes bailouts the idea that  rotting companies that exploit their  customers their employees the  environment and who knows what else are  able to receive a bailout from taxpayer  money just because the company is  quote-unquote too big to fail is  disgusting the official reasoning for  bailouts is that if these companies were  to collapse hundreds of thousands of  jobs would be lost and the subsequent  economic shock waves would be  catastrophic but maybe that's actually  better than keeping these parasitic  corporations alive  but that's besides the point the point  is that bailouts have a terrible  reputation and for good reason  however there have been a few bailouts  in history that have actually played out  surprisingly well you would think that  this would be most likely to happen with  a small company that's nimble and quick  but this actually happened with the  biggest bailout in history aig  aig was an insurance conglomerate that  was suffering insane losses so the  government came to the rescue with an  appalling at 182 billion dollars the  bailout itself is large enough to be the  world's 60th largest company now i don't  support this bailout but the us  government did end up profiting from the  bailout and that things worked out  surprisingly well  so here's why a company even needed a  182 billion dollar bailout and why the  bailout actually kind of worked  182 billion dollars is an insane amount  of money so how did a company even end  up in such a terrible position well the  answer is half a century worth of  terrible decisions that eventually  caught up aig wasn't always bad though  like most companies aig was started with  good intentions by a man named cornelius  vander starr cornelius was born to a  dutch family living in fort bragg  california in 1892 things started off  good for cornelius when he was a baby  his father had a respectable job as a  railroad engineer and the family was  able to comfortably get by but this  didn't last long as his father would  pass away when cornelius was just 2  years old  this forced the family to take in  borders to get by if you don't know what  this is it's basically converting your  house into a long-term airbnb to earn  money considering this background you  would think that cornelius would play  things safe when it came to his career  but he was always more of a risk taker  he would drop out of uc berkeley after  just one year and would try opening an  ice cream shop this was somewhat  successful but nothing crazy so he would  end up joining the us army near the end  of world war one cornelius never got  deployed but he could feel this urge to  travel and see the world so he would get  a job as a clerk at a japanese office  and move to japan from there he would  travel to shanghai china in 1919 and  this is where he would found aig though  it wasn't called aig at the time  the young company focused on providing  western insurance products to the asian  market which proved to be quite popular  soon enough cornelius had operations  across all of southeast asia along with  the philippines indonesia and malaysia  it wasn't until he had a strong presence  in asia that cornelius even opened an  office in america but even after opening  an american office cornelius didn't  really pursue the american market maybe  he thought it was too competitive his  reasoning was not too clear but  something that was clear was that aig  was phenomenal at attracting customers  in developing countries so naturally  cornelius would turn his attention to  cuba and latin america it wasn't until  world war ii hit that cornelius finally  shifted his focus to america you see it  cornelius saw an opportunity to provide  insurance for military personnel which  turned out to be extremely popular after  the war cornelius would spend his time  expanding across devastated european  countries like france italy germany and  the uk  clearly cornelius was no saint he built  aig on the backs of other people's  hardships first he leveraged people from  underdeveloped countries then he  leveraged military families and finally  he leveraged war streak in europe  cornelius was no doubt an opportunist  but something he wasn't was a fraud when  he was at the helm aig was sounded  financially i mean they literally  survived the great depression but  naturally cornelius couldn't run the  company forever so he would end up  handing the company over to a man named  maurice r greenberg also known as hank  in the 1960s and this marked the  beginning of the end  ever since hank joined aig he only had  one goal which was to impress his  superiors at first this was cornelius  and eventually this would become the  shareholders now trying to impress her  superiors isn't a bad goal per se  but the problem was that hank was  willing to do almost anything and this  was evident from the very beginning when  hank was hired in 1960 he was given the  task of developing an international  accident and health insurance business  but his approach to doing so was  completely different from what we saw  with cornelius much of cornelius's  success could be attributed to his  patients he never forced an opportunity  he was willing to wait years if not  decades for the right opportunity it  doesn't seem like hank was a fan of this  approach though as he far preferred to  create this opportunity and the way he  accomplished this was by leveraging  insurance brokers instead of insurance  agents for most of you guys this  probably sounds like the same thing but  there's a small difference you see  insurance agents are employed by the  insurance company meaning that their  loyalty lies with the company it's in  the agent's best interest to find the  best clients to ensure  insurance brokers on the other hand are  technically supposed to be loyal to the  customer but really the only thing  they're loyal to it is the commission  it's in the broker's best interest to  complete as many transactions as  possible the quality of the transaction  doesn't really matter to them so from  the company's perspective in the long  term it's better to use agents than  brokers because while agents are more  expensive they'll secure higher quality  clients and protect the liquidity of the  firm but hank threw all of this out the  window and went all in on brokers he  figured that even if sales went down the  company would be more profitable thanks  to the small commissions that brokers  charged in the short term this made him  look like a genius as his departments  were more profitable than ever but what  he was really doing was gambling the  financial integrity of the firm for more  revenue and profits and as we go further  along in the story you'll realize that  this was a common trait for aig  at first hank was simply cutting corners  here and there to inflate the company's  profits but eventually he would turn to  full-on accounting fraud in the early  2000s inc orchestrated multiple  fraudulent deals with offshore entities  to make it seem like aig's reserves were  larger than they truly were these deals  also inflated aig's profits by nearly 4  billion  when these activities came to light in  2005 inc would be ousted from aig after  leaving the company for 37 years but the  damage had already been done while hank  had succeeded in growing the company's  market cap from a few hundred million  dollars to a few hundred billion dollars  and made aig the world's largest  entrance company he had also destroyed  the company fundamentally all it would  take is one gust of wind to blow over  this house of cards and that's exactly  what happened in 2008  in the end it was two risky practices  that screwed over aig and these were  credit default swaps and securities  lending credit default swaps are pretty  complex financial instruments but for  our purposes we can just think of them  as a type of insurance if you have home  insurance and something terrible happens  to your home the entrance company would  cover the damages right well it works  the same way with credit default swaps  but instead of insuring your home to  ensure corporate debt for example let's  say gm needs more money to build a new  factory you as a lender are willing to  lend gm this money but you want to make  sure that even if gm can't pay back  their debt you get your money back so  you get insurance on this debt called a  credit default swap this way even if gm  collapses aig will pay you back a  certain percentage or even the entire  loan based on the terms of the swap but  the thing to note is that aig does not  actually have enough money to pay back  all of these swaps it's unlikely that  all of these corporations default at the  same time so aig only has to carry a  portion of the total amount to insure  now this wouldn't be a problem if these  swaps were a small portion of their  portfolio but aig had written swaps on  500 billion dollars worth of assets and  as we now know corporations were  defaulted left and right throughout 2008  meaning that aig had to start paying out  massive checks but this wasn't even what  screwed them over what screwed them over  was a downgrade in their credit rating  on september 15 2008. this downgrade  meant that aig had to put up  significantly more collateral to back up  their credit default swaps collateral  they didn't have i think you can guess  what happened next  it wasn't just credit default swaps that  were screwing over the company either  they were also getting burned with  securities lending as an insurance  company aig owned millions of shares of  stocks bonds options futures variable  annuities and basically every other  security you can think of now aig could  have just kept these securities to  themselves but aig wasn't satisfied with  the returns that the securities could  offer by themselves so they decided to  lend out these securities in return for  interest this is a pretty common  practice amongst financial institutions  and it wouldn't have been that bad if  aig stopped right here but they would of  course take it to even the next level  you see when someone borrows a security  from aig aside from paying interest on  the security they also have to give aig  cash collateral the borrower can pay  back the security at any time and ask  for their cash back so it's an aig's  best interest to keep the cash  collateral as liquid as possible but aig  decided that it would be a great idea to  invest his cash into subprime mortgages  when the financial crisis hit a lot of  aig's borrowers were trying to raise  cash for the recession so they paid back  their securities and asked ig for their  cash collateral back but you know what  this cash was invested in subprime  mortgages which um completely collapsed  so yeah it was bad  at this point it was just a matter of  time until aig needed a bailout  aig would go on to post some of the  worst corporate quarters known to  mankind on this channel we often discuss  market cap losses but the losses that  we're about to discuss are not market  cap losses they're cash losses in q3 of  2008 aig would report a net loss of 24.5  billion dollars which was the eighth  worst corporate quarter ever and that  wasn't the worst of it in q4 of 2008 aig  would lose 61.7 billion dollars which is  the worst court recorder ever reported  to this day with these types of losses  even a 182 billion dollar bailout would  only last a few quarters and that's why  they got such a massive bailout the  bailout didn't come for free though the  us government got a 79.9 stake in the  company for the first 85 billion dollars  and aig had to pay back the remaining  bailout with interest fortunately this  was enough money to sustain their losses  and get them through the recession which  is what aig really needed i mean it's  not like aig was the next radio shack or  sears their business model was still  profitable the problem was that it only  worked in bull markets so they just had  to get through the bear market and  that's exactly what the government  helped them do  as the economy recovered so did aig's  bottom line and they would become  profitable again in late 2010 this  allowed them to pay off the bailout with  interest at the same time the treasury  was slowly selling off aig stock on the  market and by the time everything was  said and done it the u.s actually ended  up profiting 22.7 billion dollars  meanwhile things that worked out pretty  well for aig as well they've downsized  significantly since their peak but  they're still worth over 40 billion  dollars so they're still pretty big  considering this happy ending some have  argued that bailouts don't have to be  bad if done correctly but in the end i  think it still comes down to a consent  issue with such bailouts the government  is essentially making everyday americans  a shareholder in these garbage companies  which is something the taxpayers never  consented to sometimes if bailouts can  work out like with aig  but by definition bailouts are handed  out to companies with the most liquidity  risk so more times than not it's not  gonna work out but even if it did i'm  still not a fan of bailouts given that  they're not approved by the people that  are funding it also i think it's  probably best if such companies go under  it anyway but that's just what i think  do you think aig should have been bailed  out comment that down below  also drop a like if you're not a fan of  bailouts either and of course consider  checking out our discord community to  suggest future video ideas and consider  subscribing to more questions logically  answered but until then i'm hari and  i'll see you guys on the next one  you
 

